    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d475">

                <pb facs="0659" n="651" sameAs="frus:frus1941v04/pg651"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.93/481½</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Lauchlin Currie,
                        Administrative Assistant to President Roosevelt, to the Secretary of State</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-11-25">November 25,
                        1941</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>I have just received a cable from Owen Lattimore<anchor n="83" xml:id="d475-f1"/>
                    in which he tells me that the Generalissimo is greatly agitated by the report
                    from the Chinese Ambassador following his conference with you. Lattimore <pb
                        facs="0660" n="652" xml:id="pg652"/> makes a point that Chiang Kai-shek’s
                    reliance on America is the foundation of his whole national policy and that this
                    would be destroyed by any loosening of economic pressure or unfreezing on our
                    part while leaving Japan entrenched in China. He draws an analogy to the closing
                    of the Burma Road, which permanently destroyed British prestige in China.
                    Lattimore remarked that he had never really seen Chiang Kai-shek agitated
                    before.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d475-f1" n="83" xml:id="d475-f1-content">
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Infra;</hi> Mr. Lattimore was American Political Adviser
                    to Generalissimo Chiang Kai-shek.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
